00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:17.730
Yeah.
是的。

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:21.009
And she's the one who suffers the consequence of their like being idiots.
而她正是他们那种幼稚行为的受害者。

00:17:21.009 --> 00:17:24.089
Yeah, she's like, are you arguing the extent to what you stabbed me?
是的，她会说，你是在跟我争论你刺伤我的程度吗？

00:17:24.089 --> 00:17:24.930
Right.
对。

00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:25.769
And I love it.
我太喜欢了。

00:17:25.769 --> 00:17:33.930
You have a great moment where your Ted is, Ted is, Marshall says, in the waiting room in the hospital, I can't believe I stabbed my fiancee.
你有一个很棒的时刻，你的泰德是，泰德是，马歇尔在医院的候诊室里说，我不敢相信我刺伤了我的未婚妻。

00:17:33.930 --> 00:17:40.529
And you have this amazing performance of a fine joke, but you made a great that was essentially a, come on, Marshall.
而你表现得非常精彩，这是一个不错的笑话，但你演得很棒，本质上就是一个“拜托，马歇尔”。

00:17:40.529 --> 00:17:42.170
Do you really think she's still your fiance?
你真的觉得她还是你的未婚妻吗？

00:17:42.170 --> 00:17:56.769
It's such a great mislead, the way you played it was so good, it was like I was like Rebecca and I were watching that, it was like a full laugh out, that was the loudest laugh of the entire episode I think was that joke in my living room.
这是一个多么棒的误导，你演得太好了，就像我和丽贝卡在看一样，简直让人捧腹大笑，我想那是我客厅里整集笑声最大的一次。

00:17:56.769 --> 00:18:20.636
So the one take that a lot of people had on this episode was less about the friendship and the growing up and making the decisions between your friends and then they get married what changes, but about the notion of the apartment that is important to you, of the land, the place that means something to you, because Lily goes through losing hers and now Ted or Marshall is potentially losing theirs.
所以很多人对这一集的一个看法，与其说是关于友谊、成长、在朋友之间做决定以及他们结婚后会发生什么变化，不如说是关于那个对你很重要的公寓的概念，关于土地，那个对你意味着某些地方的概念，因为莉莉失去了她的公寓，现在泰德或马歇尔也可能失去他们的公寓。

00:18:20.636 --> 00:18:24.106
And so I'll just read a couple of questions on this topic.
所以我会读几个关于这个话题的问题。

00:18:24.106 --> 00:18:25.780
You can take it as you will.
你们可以随意回答。

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:34.394
Nigel Underscore at Leong 07 says, at the start of the episode, Ted mentions how there's one apartment in particular that will always be theirs.
Nigel Underscore at Leong 07说，在这一集开始时，泰德提到有一间特定的公寓永远会属于他们。

00:18:34.394 --> 00:18:42.290
So the question is, is there a building that you've spent time at that you would call your own, not including your house, obviously.
所以问题是，有没有哪个建筑是你曾待过并会称之为属于你自己的地方，显然不包括你的房子。

00:18:42.290 --> 00:18:43.665
Oh, that's interesting.
哦，这很有趣。

00:18:43.665 --> 00:18:44.165
And why?
为什么呢？

00:18:44.165 --> 00:18:48.532
And then Kenny Freaking Sage says, what were your first departments like?
然后Kenny Freaking Sage说，你们的第一间公寓是什么样的？

00:18:48.532 --> 00:18:53.736
Any momentos like Sharkey or the swords that you've carried with you through the years?
有什么纪念品，比如Sharkey或者是那些你多年来一直带着的宝剑吗？

00:18:53.736 --> 00:19:04.204
And finally, Liam Doyley, 44 says, did Craig and Carter have something in their lives similar to the swords where they were important momentos for their time in New York city.
最后，Liam Doyley 44说，克雷格和卡特的生活中是否有类似宝剑那样对他们在纽约市的时光很重要的纪念品。

00:19:04.204 --> 00:19:08.391
So a place or an object that were meaningful to you growing up or now.
所以，在你们成长过程中或现在，有什么地方或物品对你们很有意义吗？

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:10.926
It's a great question.
这是一个好问题。

00:19:10.926 --> 00:19:11.970
Great questions, plural.
好问题，是复数的。

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:25.436
You know, this makes me think I've always been very sentimental about houses, apartments, like even if I'm, I remember when I got how I made a mother, I was subletting my friend Megan's apartment from college.
你知道，这让我想起我一直对房子、公寓非常多愁善感，甚至当我，我记得当我制作《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，我在转租我大学朋友梅根的公寓。

00:19:25.436 --> 00:19:26.634
It was $750 a month.
一个月750美元。

00:19:26.634 --> 00:19:31.784
It was a one-bedroom like, right, you know, a couple blocks from Kenner's deli, right?
那是一套一居室，就在离肯纳熟食店几个街区的地方，对吧？

00:19:31.784 --> 00:19:34.898
Like it was like right by LACMA and that whole area.
就像在LACMA和那一带附近。

00:19:34.898 --> 00:19:37.234
It was a great place to live, actually.
其实是个居住的好地方。

00:19:37.234 --> 00:19:41.605
I really love that apartment and my best friends lived across the street.
我真的很喜欢那间公寓，我最好的朋友就住在街对面。

00:19:41.605 --> 00:19:55.320
So it was really felt like I had community and I had, and you know, we were going into season two and it was kind of like, I don't know that I need to, I can certainly afford a little more than a seven-year-old brother apartment.
所以真的感觉我有了社群，而且你知道，我们当时正在制作第二季，就觉得，我不知道我是不是需要，我当然可以负担得起比七百多块钱更好的公寓。

00:19:55.319 --> 00:20:26.831
but my friend David had just kind of like redone it for me while I was away one summer and I came back and it was really spiffed up but I bought this really lovely house about 10, 12 minutes away and I remember being devastated that I had to leave this $750 a month apart with yellow walls and my friend Megan had a swing in it like it was weird it was a weird apartment I think David took the swing down but it was a weird apartment but it was my apartment and I had a lot of great memories there.
但是我的朋友大卫在我有一年夏天离开的时候帮我重新装修了一下，我回来时它被收拾得很漂亮，但我买了一栋距离那里大约10到12分钟路程的非常可爱的房子，我记得我当时感到非常心碎，因为我不得不离开这间月租750美元、有着黄色墙壁的公寓，我朋友梅根在里面挂了一个秋千，就像这很奇怪，这是一间奇怪的公寓，我想大卫把秋千拆了，但这是一间奇怪的公寓，不过它是我的公寓，我在那里有很多美好的回忆。

00:20:26.831 --> 00:20:31.640
And I can weave myself into any place, like I'm quite an adaptable creature.
我可以融入任何地方，我是一个相当适应力强的人。

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:35.789
And I'm selling my house in LA right now because I live in New York.
我现在正在卖掉我在洛杉矶的房子，因为我住在纽约。

00:20:35.789 --> 00:20:38.413
And I went through a real grieving process.
我经历了一个真正的悲伤过程。

00:20:38.413 --> 00:20:39.145
I'm over it.
我已经走出来了。

00:20:39.145 --> 00:20:45.614
But I went through a real grieving process when I realized I just wasn't going to have this house forever.
但当我意识到我真的不会永远拥有这栋房子时，我经历了一个真正的悲伤过程。

00:20:45.614 --> 00:20:46.712
I love this house.
我很爱这栋房子。

00:20:46.712 --> 00:20:47.871
I love where it is.
我喜欢它所在的地方。

00:20:47.871 --> 00:20:51.228
I love the hikes I go on when I walk out my front door.
我喜欢从我家前门走出去时的徒步路线。

00:20:51.228 --> 00:20:53.668
But I will say what I think is the root.
但我想说的是我认为的根源所在。

00:20:53.668 --> 00:20:56.720
This This is really going back into the childhood.
这，这真的要回到童年。

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:01.319
We lived in a little town called Berwick in Columbus, Ohio, until fourth grade when we moved to the house.
我们住在哥伦布俄亥俄州一个叫伯威克的小镇，直到四年级我们才搬到那个房子。

00:21:01.319 --> 00:21:04.279
My parents still live in in Bexley.
我父母现在仍然住在贝克斯利。

00:21:04.279 --> 00:21:09.440
In fourth grade, I remember my dad, we were packing up the house in Berwick.
在四年级的时候，我记得我爸爸，我们当时正在打包伯威克的房子。

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.960
My dad and my two sisters had gone on.
我爸爸和我的两个姐姐已经先走了。

00:21:12.960 --> 00:21:13.960
They were gone.
他们已经走了。

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:21.480
So it was my mom and I had the final push to get out of the house and lock the keys and the final pull away.
所以当时是我和我妈妈做了最后的冲刺，离开房子，锁上门，做了最后一次驱车离开。

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:23.599
I wasn't feeling particularly emotional about this.
我对这件事并没有感到特别的情绪波动。

00:21:23.599 --> 00:21:25.880
I was just like, I know my family's coming with me.
我当时只觉得，我知道我的家人会和我一起走。

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:29.920
We get in the car, my mom closes the door and she burst into tears.
我们上了车，我妈妈关上车门，突然放声大哭。

00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:35.000
Cause it was like the first house where her kids were born, you know?
因为那是她的孩子们出生的第一栋房子，你知道吗？

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:36.599
So she burst into tears.
所以她放声大哭。

00:21:36.599 --> 00:21:42.400
And then because I am looking to my mom for cues and clues about how to behave, I burst into tears.
然后因为我在看着我妈妈以寻找关于如何表现的暗示和线索，我也放声大哭。

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.779
So we're sitting in the front seat just weeping over this house.
于是我们坐在前排座位上，为这栋房子痛哭流涕。

00:21:46.779 --> 00:21:53.539
And I think that like, I just in that moment, learn like you're more in a house.
我想我当时就是在那一刻学到，你对房子的感情不止于此。

00:21:53.539 --> 00:21:56.980
Like you get sad a little bit, and then you move on.
就像你难过一会儿，然后就继续前行。

00:21:56.980 --> 00:22:12.599
But I do think there's something very special in particular about, I have a song, I'll send this to you on eulogy volume two called Real Good House, which is about the house I left this $750 a month apartment to move into and I lived there for 14 years.
但我确实认为有一些非常特别的事情，我有首歌，我会把这个发给你，在《悼词》第二卷里，叫做《真正的好房子》，那是关于我离开这个每月750美元的公寓搬进去的房子，我在那里住了14年。

00:22:13.779 --> 00:22:15.819
And then moved to this other house.
然后搬到了这栋别的房子。

00:22:15.819 --> 00:22:20.140
And I wrote a song called Real Good House that is like a love song to a house.
我写了一首歌叫《真正的好房子》，就像是对房子的情歌。

00:22:20.140 --> 00:22:28.980
If you want, if you've ever listened to whoever wrote this, go to Yolo G volume to Josh Radder, listen to a real good house because it is literally a love song about real estate.
如果你愿意，如果你听过写这首歌的人，去听听《悼词》第二卷乔希·拉德纳的《真正的好房子》，因为它字面意思就是一首关于房地产的情歌。

00:22:28.980 --> 00:22:37.160
This old house, this good old house is living mass and breaking stone.
这栋老房子，这栋美好的老房子，承载着过往的岁月，历经风雨沧桑。

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:40.640
It's a love song about a house.
这是一首献给房子的情歌。

00:22:40.640 --> 00:23:00.450
And I think I'm really, I'm really keyed into personally just like loving spaces and feeling that there's something whole, once you've inhabited a space for a while and had enough life happened in a space and and woke up enough and gone to sleep enough in a space and felt the tides of life roll through a space enough.
我觉得我个人真的、真的对这种事很有共鸣，就是单纯地热爱空间，觉得当你在一个空间里居住了一段时间，经历足够多的生活，在里面醒来足够多次、入睡足够多次，并且充分感受过生活的潮起潮落之后，整个空间就会变得完整。

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:07.839
It's very hard to not associate yourself with that space, and it's very hard to not mourn that space when you leave it.
你很难不把你自己和那个空间联系在一起，而且当你离开它时，你很难不为之感到悲伤。

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:09.968
Yeah, well said.
是的，说得好。

00:23:09.968 --> 00:23:21.599
I mean, my meta answer to this question for me is I was just thinking about what stage 22 was to us all on the Voxlot, where we shot nine seasons of How I Met Your Mother.
我的意思是，对于这个问题，我内心的终极回答是，我刚刚一直在想22号片场对我们所有人在福克斯外景地（Voxlot）意味着什么，我们在那里拍摄了《老爸老妈浪漫史》的九季节目。

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:27.760
And that feeling of not only is it leaving somewhere, but you're going, someone else is going to come in here.
那种感觉不仅仅是离开某个地方，而是你会意识到，别人要搬进来了。

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:32.244
There's going to be some other TV show that they'll be like, ah, that's our stage, stage 22.
会有别的什么电视剧组，他们会说，啊，那是我们的片场，22号片场。

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:33.804
It's like, no, that's our stage.
你会觉得，不对，那是我们的片场。

00:23:33.804 --> 00:23:37.410
But that's the circle of life, and it's the same thing with houses, right?
但这就是生命的循环，房子也是一样，对吧？

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:43.648
I mean, these, yeah, Carter and I had a couple of apartments in New York that were sort of like, we had this one on 75th Street.
我是说，这些，卡特和我以前在纽约有几套公寓，差不多就是那种，我们在75街有一套。

00:23:43.648 --> 00:23:50.958
That was kind of what we imagined was the Marshall and Ted apartment in a way, although it didn't bear tons of resemblance to it really in the design.
在某种程度上，那就是我们想象中的马歇尔和泰德的公寓，尽管从设计上来说，它其实跟剧里长得不太一样。

00:23:50.958 --> 00:23:53.103
It was kind of like that would have been it.
但感觉就像那是我们的公寓。

00:23:53.103 --> 00:23:58.074
And yeah, I remember letting go of that apartment to sort of go like I was getting married and Carter.
是的，我还记得为了准备结婚而退掉那套公寓的情景，当时卡特……

00:23:58.074 --> 00:24:04.019
But I wasn't leaving the apartment to get married or to move into an apartment with my wife and I hadn't gotten there yet.
但当时我不是为了结婚或跟妻子搬进公寓而离开那套公寓，因为我还没走到那一步。

00:24:04.019 --> 00:24:15.960
But I remember the feeling of moving out from Carter, I had lived with Carter for a few years after college writing for Letterman and were just these idiots in our 20s, me, the bigger idiot, because Carter had provided as I learned when I moved.
但我记得从卡特那里搬出来的感觉，大学毕业后我和卡特一起住了几年，当时我们在为莱特曼（Letterman）写剧本，就是二十多岁的一帮傻小子，我呢，是那个更傻的，因为正如我搬家时所了解到的那样，很多东西都是卡特准备的。

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:22.720
The day I moved downtown to the West Village was from the Upper West Side to my own place in the West Village, the movers brought in everything.
我搬到市中心西村的那天，是从上西区搬到我在西村的新住处，搬运工把所有东西都搬了进来。

00:24:22.720 --> 00:24:28.380
And that was the only event that I realized how little furniture I had and how much of it had been Carter.
也就是在那一刻，我才意识到我的家具是多么少，其中有多少其实都是卡特的。

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:32.759
So I really didn't occur to me till he got there and I was like, oh, I don't have a couch.
所以直到他把东西搬到那里，我才反应过来，心想：哦，我连个沙发都没有。

00:24:32.759 --> 00:24:34.839
I guess I'll just sit in this one share.
我想我只能坐在这一把椅子上了。

00:24:34.839 --> 00:24:38.039
And it was just one of those moments where I was like, God, I'm really immature.
那真是一个让我觉得“天哪，我怎么这么不成熟”的时刻。

00:24:38.039 --> 00:24:40.920
I'm really like a lot of, I'm just a kid still in some way.
我真的觉得自己就像……在某种程度上依然是个孩子。

00:24:40.920 --> 00:24:43.079
I'm like, there's just no, there's no, oh, that stuff.
我心想，怎么会没有，怎么会没有……那些东西呢？

00:24:43.079 --> 00:24:43.920
Where did that go?
那些东西去哪了？

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:44.759
It was Carter's things.
那都是卡特的东西。

00:24:44.759 --> 00:24:46.440
And he took it to his new apartment.
他把它们带到了他的新公寓。

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:50.160
So he was clearly more together than I was, than I was.
所以他显然比我成熟得多，比我懂生活得多。

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:51.759
And I didn't go out and buy all this furniture.
而我根本没有跑出去买齐这些家具。

00:24:51.759 --> 00:24:55.079
Cause I was like, oh, it just didn't magically appear here.
因为我想，哦，它们不会就这么凭空出现。

00:24:55.079 --> 00:24:59.000
But yeah, those places in your 20s, they have a kind of meaning.
但说真的，二十多岁时住过的那些地方，它们都带有某种特殊的意义。

00:24:59.000 --> 00:25:04.039
And those, you know, you'll never live quite like that again.
而且那些日子，你知道的，你再也不会那样生活了。

00:25:04.039 --> 00:25:09.480
And yeah, I think that one on 75th Street for Carter and I would be like, that's the Marshall and Ted apart.
所以，我和卡特在75街的那套房子，对我们来说就像是——那是马歇尔和泰德的公寓。

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:22.240
And also, it occurs to me that in the grand, if you really zoom up and take in the grand perspective, like, there's no such thing as a home forever, like a forever home, you know?
而且我还意识到，从宏观的角度来看，如果你跳脱出来，从长远的大局去审视，你会发现，根本没有什么永远的家，也就是所谓的“终极之所”，对吧？

00:25:22.240 --> 00:25:25.960
They will, you know, they'll all go away.
它们都会，你知道的，它们终将消失。

00:25:25.960 --> 00:25:30.119
I mean, at some point, you're probably gonna want to move into a home that doesn't have stairs, you know what I mean?
我的意思是，到了某个阶段，你可能就会想搬进一栋没有楼梯的房子里，你知道我的意思吗？

00:25:30.119 --> 00:25:34.839
Like, you've got to adapt your notion of what home is.
所以，你必须调整你对“家”这个概念的理解。

00:25:34.839 --> 00:25:42.039
like, I try not to talk too much about my relationship on here, but it comes up.
我尽量不在节目里多谈我的感情生活，但它总是会不经意地冒出来。

00:25:42.039 --> 00:26:00.880
But I had this feeling about Jordana when I was falling in love with her and that I was pretty sure we were going to get married, is she had this feeling that I knew wherever we went, it would feel like home, like us together felt like home, not the structure of wherever we were.
当我和约尔达娜（Jordana）坠入爱河，并且确信我们俩会结婚时，我曾经有过这样一种感觉：她有一种魔力，让我知道无论我们走到哪里，都会觉得像是在家里，只要我们在一起就有了家的感觉，而不是取决于我们身处什么样的建筑结构中。

00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:01.880
That's it.
就是这样。

00:26:01.880 --> 00:26:04.400
That's all home can ever be, I think really, right?
我想，这其实就是家所能成为的全部意义，对吧？

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:08.920
And that needs to be a moveable feast to go all hemming way about it.
用海明威（Hemingway）的话来说，这需要是一个“流动的盛宴”。

00:26:08.920 --> 00:26:11.200
And you need, it needs to be, we'll have to travel.
我们需要它能够随身携带，我们需要去旅行。

00:26:11.480 --> 00:26:22.799
And you need sometimes, I mean, hopefully not for horrifying geopolitical reasons, but sometimes you got to pick up and go, you know, sometimes you got to like, it's where you get a job or like, we have to be adaptable enough.
有时你需要——当然最好不是因为那些可怕的地缘政治原因——但有时你确实得收拾行李出发，你知道的，有时你得因为找到工作之类的事情而搬迁，我们需要具备足够的适应能力。

00:26:22.799 --> 00:26:28.960
And again, this comes back to this notion of we're kind of fusing the two great themes that we've talked about in the dual episodes, right?
再说一次，这又回到了我们前面讨论过的双集主题——我们在某种程度上融合了那两个伟大的主题，对吧？

00:26:29.319 --> 00:26:34.119
But like this notion of change being this constant force resisting it.
一方面是“变化是一种恒定力量，而我们却在抗拒它”。

00:26:34.400 --> 00:27:01.265
but also getting for it will win it will always win yes and then this notion of home being a not quite a permanent thing although you know one of the things that people always get upset about if you live in new york city and then you watch shows about new york city that the apartments are too big for the income bracket of the people living in them yeah There's a Peter Hedges movie that our friend Katie Holmes started and called pieces of April, which if you haven't seen that movie is a fantastic movie.
但同时又明白，变化终究会获胜，它永远会赢，是的；另一方面则是“家”并不是一个绝对永久的事物——尽管你知道，如果你住在纽约市，然后看那些以纽约市为背景的电视剧，人们总是会吐槽说，里面的公寓对于剧中人物的收入水平来说实在是太大了，对，皮特·赫奇斯（Peter Hedges）导演过一部电影，我们的朋友凯特·哈德森（译者注：此处台词提及Katie Holmes，即凯特·霍尔姆斯的误读）主演的，叫《四角lounge》（Pieces of April，译者注：片名直译为《四月碎片》），如果你没看过那部电影，它真的是一部非常棒的电影。

00:27:01.265 --> 00:27:03.119
I remember them in the movie, yeah.
我记得他们在电影里演的情节，对。

00:27:03.119 --> 00:27:06.880
Patty Clarkson, Oliver Platt, John Gallagher, Alison Pilt, great movie.
帕蒂·克拉克森（Patty Clarkson）、奥利弗·普莱特（Oliver Platt）、约翰·加拉赫（John Gallagher）、艾莉森·皮尔（Alison Pill），非常棒的电影。

00:27:10.799 --> 00:27:14.389
And the apartment in there is like, I don't know how they filmed it.
里面的那个公寓，我真不知道他们是怎么在里面拍摄的。

00:27:14.389 --> 00:27:19.509
And the reason that you can't have super small apartments is because you can't fit a whole thing.
之所以你不能把公寓弄得超级小，是因为你根本塞不下拍摄所需的全部设备。

00:27:19.509 --> 00:27:21.040
It's really hard to shoot in.
那种地方拍戏真的很难施展开。

00:27:21.039 --> 00:27:22.409
It's really hard to shoot.
要在里面拍摄实在太难了。

00:27:22.409 --> 00:27:25.884
But they made that apartment look very small and very appropriate.
但他们把那间公寓拍得看起来非常小，而且非常符合实际情况。

00:27:25.884 --> 00:27:33.887
But there is something about this Upper West Side apartment in 2005 that I do buy that through some real estate miracle stroke of luck in New York City.
不过，对于2005年那套上西区的公寓，我确实相信，凭借纽约市里某种房地产方面的奇迹或运气，

00:27:33.887 --> 00:27:39.627
They would have been able to get something that's not completely implausible and the way we tended to it too.
他们确实有可能租到一处并非完全不合情理的房子，这也是我们处理布景时的一种倾向。

00:27:39.627 --> 00:27:42.786
It's a little bigger maybe than the way they could have got.
它可能比他们现实中真正能租到的要稍微大一点。

00:27:42.786 --> 00:27:50.000
But we said to our wonderful set designer who won multiple Emmys, Steve Olson, we said, it's probably still going to be a little too big.
但我们对曾多次荣获艾美奖的优秀布景设计师史蒂夫·奥尔森（Steve Olson）说，

00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:51.403
Let's not make it crazy big.
它可能还是会稍微有点大。

00:27:51.403 --> 00:27:54.559
I know it has to be a little big to shoot in and for logistics.
但我们别把它搞得大得离谱。

00:27:54.559 --> 00:28:04.663
but let's scuff it up, let's have the paint be not perfect and bright and amazing and not look like a set for a Hollywood show being shot in Los Angeles about New York.
我知道为了拍摄和后勤调度，它必须得稍微大一点，但让我们把它弄得有点旧感，让油漆别那么完美、鲜亮和惊艳，别看起来像是好莱坞在洛杉矶搭建的、用来假装纽约的片场。

00:28:04.663 --> 00:28:16.271
You know, I remember what season it was, but I don't think this was a set design thing, but there was a screw that was sticking out of the door and everyone kept like nicking their thumb on it.
我不记得那是第几季了，我觉得这应该不是布景设计的一部分，但当时门上有一颗突出的螺丝钉，每个人走过时都会不小心把大拇指给刮破。

00:28:16.271 --> 00:28:21.262
And we had to keep asking them to screw it in because everyone was getting injured.
我们不得不一直要求他们把螺丝拧进去，因为总有人受伤。

00:28:21.262 --> 00:28:24.450
You know, you bust in and out of those doors so fast.
你知道的，大家进出那些门的速度都太快了。

00:28:24.450 --> 00:28:24.991
Oh, yeah.
哦，是的。

00:28:24.991 --> 00:28:26.194
That's really funny.
这太搞笑了吧。

00:28:26.194 --> 00:28:28.479
I don't think I ever knew that detail.
我以前好像从不知道这个细节。

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:32.688
I just want to say, by the way, that Steve Olson is a listener to this show.
顺便说一句，我只想说，史蒂夫·奥尔森也是这个节目的听众。

00:28:32.688 --> 00:28:34.072
He comments on Instagram.
他在Instagram上留言。

00:28:34.072 --> 00:28:36.010
Steve Olson, the great Steve Olson.
史蒂夫·奥尔森，伟大的史蒂夫·奥尔森。

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:39.222
I don't think it was Steve's fault, the screw on the door.
门上的那颗螺丝应该不是史蒂夫的错。

00:28:39.222 --> 00:28:40.108
Steve was great.
史蒂夫真的很棒。

00:28:40.108 --> 00:28:50.185
Steve lived in New York, and he lived in New York for a lot of years too, and he actually lived right next to the late show with David Letterman studio, like the Ed Sullivan Theater.
史蒂夫住在纽约，而且他在纽约住了很多年，他实际上就住在《大卫·莱特曼深夜秀》工作室的隔壁，也就是埃德·沙利文剧院（Ed Sullivan Theater）附近。

00:28:50.185 --> 00:28:55.280
And when we said we want to make McLarens kind of like the bar McGee's, have you been there?
当我们说我们想把麦克拉伦酒吧（McLarens）弄得有点像现实中的麦吉酒吧（McGee's）时，我们问他：你去过那儿吗？

00:28:55.280 --> 00:28:57.605
And he said, I've been there 10,000 times.
他说：我去过一万次了。

00:28:57.605 --> 00:28:59.986
I lived right around the corner from there.
我就住在离那儿转角不远的地方。

00:28:59.986 --> 00:29:03.696
He was even featured on a Dave Letterman meet the neighbor segment.
他甚至还上过大卫·莱特曼的“认识邻居”节目录制环节。

00:29:03.696 --> 00:29:06.409
You know, Dave would just send his camera around.
你知道的，大卫会把摄像机派出去采访。

00:29:06.409 --> 00:29:08.348
They knocked on Steve Olson's door.
他们敲响了史蒂夫·奥尔森的家门。

00:29:08.348 --> 00:29:13.940
And Steve was on a live show with Dave Letterman, like talking to Dave down the studio on the camera.
然后史蒂夫就通过摄像头，直接跟远在演播室大卫·莱特曼连线对话了。

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:23.519
Like he was so when we said we were trying to hire a set designer, the fact that Steve knew the bars that kept the murals, the red boots, I know exactly we're talking about.
所以他当时……当我们说我们在寻找布景设计师时，史蒂夫对那些酒吧的了解——那些保留的壁画、红色的靴子——让我们一拍即合，我说：“我知道你在说什么。”

00:29:23.519 --> 00:29:26.240
So it was one of those destiny moments when we met Steve.
所以当我们遇到史蒂夫时，那真是一种命中注定的时刻。

00:29:26.240 --> 00:29:28.559
So shout out to Steve Olson.
所以，我们要向史蒂夫·奥尔森致敬。

00:29:28.559 --> 00:29:34.470
And yeah, one of the many amazing people that came to create the universe of him, yeah.
是的，他是创造这个宇宙的众多了不起的人之一，是的。

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:36.029
All right, well, thank you all so much again.
好的，非常感谢大家再次收听。

00:29:36.029 --> 00:29:43.269
Great questions everywhere, send them to YouTube and Instagram and on our website how we made your mother's slash contact.
到处都有非常棒的问题，请把它们发送到YouTube、Instagram以及我们的网站 howwemyourmothers.com/contact。

00:29:43.269 --> 00:29:46.920
And we'll see you next time.
我们下期再见。

00:29:46.920 --> 00:29:49.349
I am guilty.
我有罪。

00:29:49.349 --> 00:29:53.509
Please equip me, all sins are forgiven.
请赦免我吧，一切罪孽皆可被宽恕。

00:29:53.509 --> 00:29:55.990
In New York City.
在纽约市。

